Subject: [PATCH] ctrl: one lock for the controller's data model, taken first by every thread

The controller's threads shared the agents' models with no common lock: the nodes (one thread per
radio, several on one agent's model), the main loop (the decoders, the orchestrator, the 250 ms
database pass, the retire passes, the DataElements getters forwarded to it), the nodes listener
(frame routing, which reads models, sets node states and makes nodes) and the bus threads (the
seven TR-181 methods and the topology getter, which read the models). Station rows were added and
deleted under the topology lock, the command lock, or neither, and read under neither: a row or a
model freed under a reader is a use-after-free, and two walks of one map move its shared cursor.

The orchestrator's command lock is now the controller's one lock (em_mgr_t::lock_data_model, none
on the agent, which takes it in its own handlers). Every thread takes it first: each node around
each frame and tick (em_t::proto_run, now for both services), the main loop around each event and
each pass of the ticks (run_locked_step), the nodes listener around each frame it routes
(em_mgr_t::proto_process) and the bus handlers for their run (through the controller instance
they read the models of, get_em_ctrl_instance). The other locks (topology, liveness,
backhaul, autoconfiguration, node map, queues) are taken inside it, so their own orders no longer
matter between these threads. start_complete runs outside it, as before: its bus registration,
ControllerID set and subscription wait for the bus, whose thread may be waiting for the lock.
Under the lock the main thread only publishes on the bus, which waits for no reply.

No thread holding it waits for another controller thread. Freeing a node did: free_nodes joins the
node's thread and waits for passes of the listener and the other nodes, which may be waiting for
the lock. A node deleted in a locked step (a Reset's delete_nodes) is now told to stop in the step
and freed after it (free_deferred_nodes); a node stopped while it waited for the lock skips the
frame or tick it waited for, so it never runs on a model the step freed after deleting it.

The cost is latency: a node's frame waits while another thread finishes its step, the database
pass's SQL included. A main-loop step that held the lock for EM_LOCKED_STEP_LOG_MS (1 s) or longer,
and a node that waited that long for it, are logged.

Tests: locked_step_frees_the_nodes_it_deleted_after_the_lock (a node waiting for the lock and
deleted in a step: the step ends in well under EM_NODE_JOIN_MS and the node is freed after it);
bus_methods_and_frame_routing_wait_for_the_lock (the seven method handlers and the listener's
routing make no progress while another thread holds the lock, and all run once it is let go);
station_rows_change_while_the_bus_and_the_listener_read (the main thread's steps put and remove an
agent's station rows while a bus thread encodes the topology with its station lists and the
listener routes the agent's Topology Responses: clean under ASan and ThreadSanitizer).
